{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall remember, etc.</b> These are the words of Moses, and the intention is that the Sabbath day also contains an prompting for a person to remember the Exodus from Egypt: when he rests without subjection, he will feel that HaShem is the One who grants rest to His people from all their oppressors, and he will set his heart to accept the decrees of the One who brings forth from servitude. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Pesaḥim 117b): from here that one must mention the Exodus from Egypt in the sanctification of the day." "<b>And if</b> you would say: why did he omit the reason for the Sabbath that HaShem stated in the first set of tablets, which is ״כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים״ etc.? — that reason is the reason of the commandment in its essence, not the reason for the obligation, and Moses stated the reason that establishes faith in the reason of the commandment, based on what our Sages of blessed memory said and we wrote in another place, that the Exodus from Egypt revealed the faith in the creation of the world (Shemot Rabbah 12), and this is what he signified by saying <b>and you shall remember</b> etc. <b>and He brought you out</b> etc. <b>with a mighty hand and an outstretched arm</b> — and there you saw that He is the Master of all, and through this the reason stated in the first tablets, ״כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים״ etc., will be verified for you."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:27.171354551Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}